ref 17592186045436] [17592186049372 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429620 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049372 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429621 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049372 #edb/ref 1031 "Properties of Transformed Secant and Cosecant Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049372 #edb/ref 1033 778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049372 #edb/ref 1034 1.1244524594764365 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049372 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "ef892592-f698-57d9-99c9-6f0ba78e5060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186152218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1031 "Local Extrema of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1033 2707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050033 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "27991def-afa0-5245-84f6-cb161dd1a2bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186059670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Points on Transformed Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1033 141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1034 0.9516829341638788 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050713 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "60a435b6-b7ba-5949-9c3d-ce4c96d37141" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13411" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059434 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=x^4.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y = (x-2)^4-6?$ \n\nI. $(a-2, b-6)$\n\nII. $(a+2,b+ 6)$\n\nIII. $(a+2, b-6)$\n\nIV. $(a-2,b+ 6)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2138def0-a28a-5018-b93f-c63022825ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059434 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = (x-2)^4-6,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=x^4.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-2)^4.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $6$ units down and obtain the graph of $y = (x-2)^4-6.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+2,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+2,b-6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe are told that the point $(a, b)$ lies on the graph of $y = x^4$, which means:\n\n$$\nb=a^4\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find which point must lie on the graph of:\n\n$$\ny = (x-2)^4-6\n$$\n\nLet’s test what happens when we substitute $x = a + 2$ into this new equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+2-2)^{4}-6 \\\\  & ={a}^{4}-6 \\\\  & =b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $x = a + 2$, we get $y = b - 6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059435 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059435 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x) - 1.$ What point **must** lie on $y = -f(x) + 3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55fd531a-92e2-5f75-8c40-585d4f71bf4c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059435 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y = f(x) - 1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,0)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,0+1)=(3,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1)) = (3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-1+3)=(3,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3, 0)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-1 = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nf(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ Substituting $x = 3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+3 \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3, 2)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059436 #edb/ref 1069 "e-13413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059436 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d12a03b0100b460301fa0a235ce73be995594091fdace654b5a3b9ada6aa259e.png)\n\n \n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0969b02-52c8-5d22-b3e0-b594d9fbd4d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059436 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(2x)-1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis, and then\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y = f(2x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,6).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the result along the $x$-axis by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=f(2x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 2, 6 \\right) = (1,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit downward and get the graph of $y = f(2x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,6-1) = (1,5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(2x)-1$ is $(1,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186256541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517145 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517154 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517159 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517169 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186555831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059668 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186517170 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059668 #ed