ef 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186060177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290289 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517098 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517102 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186517103 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1040 #edb/ref 17592186517119 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186059436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059670 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186060522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding Points on Transformed Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186060516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186059878 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2f099e95-7371-5eb4-a3d6-9210e6669092" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060516 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060516 #edb/ref 1061 118630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060516 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "17aa209f-3415-50cd-b096-6597119d3022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060516 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060517 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060517 #edb/ref 1061 118631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060517 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad5aa1ab-d653-5e01-bdaa-28b12af3c39a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060517 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060518 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060518 #edb/ref 1061 118634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060518 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8380c8c1-7231-5bd4-9a45-21163a2d4d95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060518 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060519 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060519 #edb/ref 1061 118637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060519 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "47250ab3-5cd2-5ed3-b989-5e178c31e80e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060519 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060520 #edb/ref 1061 118638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060520 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0eda5d7c-05ca-54a9-9399-5a17533e7389" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060520 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060521 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060521 #edb/ref 1061 118832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060521 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d663df39-763f-5675-9918-5cb19be43eb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060521 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060522 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060522 #edb/ref 1061 118929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060522 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5a7667c3-64c1-5b24-95d5-8fa128b323bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060522 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186061000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060996 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we know that the point $( - 1 , 2 )$ lies on the graph of $y = f ( x ).$ What point *must* lie on the graph of $y = - f ( x ) + 3?$ This problem can be solved using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1 - Transform the Point Using Graph Transformations**\n\nTo plot $y = -f(x) + 3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(2)) = (-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result by $3$ units up and obtain the graph of $y = -f(x) + 3.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-2+3)={\\color{blue}(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\n**Method 2 - Evaluate the Transformed Function**\n\nSince the point $(-1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x),$ we have that $f(-1) = 2.$\n\nNow, consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+3.$ Substituting $x = {\\color{blue}-1}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+3 \\\\  & =-(2)+3 \\\\  & =-2+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $\\color{blue}(-1, 1)$ must lie on $y = -f(x) + 3.$\n\nSo, both methods give the same result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060996 #edb/ref 1065 8400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060996 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1195d1cb-20e5-539e-beee-dfafe91b6953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060996 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060998 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ shown below. \n\n![](images/af/afe8baa8375ceaf4974b62549e4f073d0e6cecfcfc1a50a91200421296141ef1.png)\n\n \n\nThis function has a local minimum point, and a local maximum point, as shown. \n\nNotice that if we perform the graph transformation $y=f(2x)-1,$ then \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *maximum* point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local *minimum* point on the transformed curve.\n\n![](images/cd/cda6d2c00de091df0f4df03d4b63019fd7c5681159cff65ea25e7518f5e263cd.png)\n\n \n\nSo, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to maximums and minimums (local and global) to minimums. \n\nIs this always the case? In other words, does *any* graph transformation map maximums to maximums and minimums to minimums? \n\nTo answer this, let's consider another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060998 #edb/ref 1065 9005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060998 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d8a626b6-4914-5cb4-b571-7c88d5ccb12b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060998 #edb/ref 1067 "Mapping Maxima and Minima" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060999 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the transformation $y=-f(x)+2,$ which contains a reflection about the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/7f/7f4041c17b278b0e40e31abbd98e3c47a8462b14a6109123d600c2db0e5d6f36.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n- the local *maximum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *minimum* ** point on the transformed curve, and\n\n- the local *minimum* point on the original graph is mapped to a local ** *maximum* ** point on the transformed curve.\n\nSo, due to the presence of vertical reflection, this transformation maps maximums (local and global) to minimums and minimums (local and global) to maximums." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060999 #edb/ref 1065 9037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060999 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "47145fae-178c-5567-b04b-16626d9cdea4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060999 #edb/ref 1067 "Cases With Vertical Reflection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061000 #edb/ref 1064 "We can summarize our findings by the following theorem:\n\n*If a transformation does **not** contain a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) maximums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) minimums. *\n\nIn the case where we do have a vertical reflection, we have the following theorem.\n\n*If a transformation contains a reflection across the $x$-axis, then the transformation maps local (or global) maximums to local (or global) minimums, and maps local (or global) minimums to local (or global) maximums. *" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061000 #edb/ref 1065 9038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061000 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "38b1a98c-0588-5659-a4e5-2478a05f7a93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061000 #edb/ref 1067 "A Summary" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233534 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233534 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233534 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233534 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3f(x+2)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a80933c-10f7-51d8-9b1a-039e3550e2d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233534 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3f(x+2),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $2$ unit to the left and get the graph of \n$y=f(x+2).$  After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-2,1)=(-3,1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the y-axis and get the graph of \n$y=3f(x+2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,3\\cdot 1)=(-3,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-1)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3f(x+2).$ Substituting $x=-3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3f(-3+2) \\\\  & =3f(-1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-3,3)$ must lie on $y=3f(x+2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233535 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233535 #edb/ref 1071 "The point  $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x-1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=f(x)+2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b6d8898-251c-5832-a715-c1e0b2d67196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233535 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(0,3)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x-1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(0,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(0-1,3)=(-1,3).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,3)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,3+2)=(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(0,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x-1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(0-1)=3\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=3.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(-1)+2 \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x)+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233626 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233626 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233626 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233626 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/282e65324ea1e7893da25ead1cc822c7c166df880c3aecc60face9adbbb7279a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233626 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "626e7d97-1fd4-5fea-b684-7fc6a67422d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233626 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $3$ units to the left, and then\n\n- translating it by $2$ unit down.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,6),$ the local maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=f(x+3)-2,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,6).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $3$ units to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+3).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-3,6)=(-5,6).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units downward and get the graph of $y=f(x+3)-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(-5,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-5,6-2)=(-5,4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local maximum of $y=f(x+3)-2$ is $(-5,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288329 #edb/ref 1080 "(3,-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288329 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288329 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d268d2d-7fd3-5193-ace2-27766687167e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288576 #edb/ref 1080 "(-7,-2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288576 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288576 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4eb982b3-78f3-5bb2-88d8-08e1c9cc7f14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289314 #edb/ref 1080 "(-7,-8)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289314 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289314 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c1b214ed-c977-5da8-9b98-583c8599dbc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289386 #edb/ref 1080 "(3,-8)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289386 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289386 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc01c367-7b62-52ca-b3f4-660a5da6d436" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289624 #edb/ref 1080 "(-1,1)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289624 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186289624 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f0cb2914-db63-5a0a-b325-34eb1a5b726a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186288576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186289624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186288329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290176 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cd3166ba-184a-5116-9d35-4f34e933136f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290285 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186290176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290285 #edb/ref 1069 "q-211288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290285 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290285 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e2903fa-7395-523d-a538-0dfcbb4ba792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290285 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right, and then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+5,-4)=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we stretch the result along the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ and get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4))=(3,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=\\frac{1}{2}f(x-5)$ is $(3,-2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186517098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517118 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517098 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1e/1eb29eb3eb3627e24a23d7315295a1d889308d67895f9b798dbf9dd71ba226df.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the local minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the local minimum of $y=f(x-2)+1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0012d1f3-80aa-5b68-8b2b-0c91a3fcab4f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517098 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- translating it by $2$ units to the right.\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit up.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the local minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the local minimum of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,1),$ the local minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation.\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-2)+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,1).$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $2$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1+2,1)=(1,1).\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x-2)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,1+1)=(1,2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the local minimum of $y=f(x-2)+1$ is $(1,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517109 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517102 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global maximum of $y=4f(x-5)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26d65a2e-856c-5dbc-a557-be478c1e06a4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517102 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=4f(x-5)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- translating it by $5$ units to the right.\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global maximum of $y=f(x)$ to the global maximum of $y=4f(x-5).$ So, we need to find the image of $(2,2),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(x-5),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(2,2).$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $5$ units to the right and get the graph of $y=f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2+5,2)=(7,2).\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by stretching the graph by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=4f(x-5).$ After this transformation, the point $(7,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(7,4\\cdot (2))=(7,8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global maximum of $y=4f(x-5)$ is $(7,8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517114 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517103 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/09/09193a765398280ec967a89b97b6dbdf2fb0700e0bb0d8157f966cda5c7534f9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=2f(x+1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3098f894-dc2f-57e8-ace9-9f4b9aa94b09" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517103 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the graph of $y=2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by\n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left.\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis.\n\nSince these transformations do not flip the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global minimum of $y=f(x)$ to the global minimum of $y=2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-2,-4),$ the global minimum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation.\n\nTo plot $y=2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-2,-4).$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by translating the graph by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2-1,-4)=(-3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the transformation by stretching the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-3,2\\cdot (-4))=(-3,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=2f(x+1)$ is $(-3,-8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517119 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186059670.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517119 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "f0c0000e-9b7d-53a8-ab7f-f52c5585c4b4" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517127 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517133 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(1,2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b55e01e5-9c24-510f-825f-e89bf23a1ddf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517133 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(1,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,3\\cdot (2))=(1,6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-(6))=(1,-6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-6-4)=(1,-10).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(1)=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(1)=6.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(1)-4\\\\&=-(6)-4\\\\&=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,-10)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517132 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517134 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(-7,8)$ lies on the curve $y=2f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b988807a-34eb-52af-995a-9e3c8a1f0e2f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517134 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-7,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=2f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (8))=(-7,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-(4))=(-7,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-4-4)=(-7,-8).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-7,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n2f(-7)=8\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-7)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-7)-4\\\\&=-(4)-4\\\\&=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-7,-8)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517128 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517140 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(-8,7)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-5?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8cb0f82-4ced-5ad3-8499-c17a38448a7a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517140 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-8,7)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-5:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,7)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (7))=(-8,\\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,\\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-(\\frac{7}{4}))=(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,- \\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}-5)=(-8,- \\frac{27}{4}).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,7)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n4f(-8)=7\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-8)=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ Substituting $x=-8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-8)-5\\\\&=-(\\frac{7}{4})-5\\\\&=- \\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517141 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186059669.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517141 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "bd32fee7-3163-5e9a-a7e1-90f7b06d904d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517162 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517145 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(-8,8)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-7?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3d089253-9314-5487-9a48-4dddc95358e7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517145 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-7,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $7$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-7.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,-8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-15).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(-8)=8.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-7.$ Substituting $x=-8$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=-f(-8)-7 \\\\ &=-( 8 )-7 \\\\ &=-8-7 \\\\ &=-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,-15)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517152 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517154 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y=3^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=3^{x/2}-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66562107-64e5-5b2b-99fc-50f565b62c27" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517154 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=3^{x/2}-2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=3^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=3^{x/2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=3^{x/2}-2.$ After this transformation, the point $(2a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2a,b-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y=3^{x}$, we have that $3^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=3^{x/2}-2.$ Substituting $x=2a$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &=3^{\\frac{2a}{2}}-2 \\\\ &=3^{a}-2 \\\\ &=b-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2a,b-2)$ must lie on $y=3^{x/2}-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517148 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517159 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(-1,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+7?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a93e41a-bed8-5a57-8ad1-e50ce41e47c6" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186517159 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+7,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-2).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $7$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+7.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f