f 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555713 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "924e4a7c-1cbd-5e7e-b4cd-c1950cd60c74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186555204 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186555126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555719 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9baf028c-2be1-525c-9089-58ae2e463aa6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555806 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555806 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555806 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555806 #edb/ref 1071 "The point  $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x+1).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+5?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555806 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55975c7c-e140-5b83-8d6a-51f167ef8423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555806 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-2,-1)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+5:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x+1).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally $1$ unit to the right  and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2+1,-1)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+5,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,-1)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(-1,-(-1))=(-1,1).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,1)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(-1,1+5)=(-1,6).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-2,-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x+1)$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(-2+1)=-1\\,⟹\\,f(-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+5.$ Substituting $x=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(-1)+5 \\\\  & =-(-1)+5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,6)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555831 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186555712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555831 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555831 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555831 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $(a,b)$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{x}.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y={e}^{2x}-3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555831 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8aef3688-a7db-5a18-95f7-9c000401998b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186555831 #edb/ref 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y={e}^{2x}-3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis and get the graph of  $y={e}^{2x}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{a}{2},b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $3$ units down and obtain the graph of $y={e}^{2x}-3.$ After this transformation, the point $(\\frac{a}{2},b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{a}{2},b-3).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={e}^{x}$, we have that ${e}^{a}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y={e}^{2x}-3.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{a}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2\\cdot (a/2)}-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{a}-3 \\\\  & =b-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{a}{2},b-3)$ must lie on $y={e}^{2x}-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
